Installing wincan
=================

wincan is two programs: candled, the candle server, which runs on Linux (Debian, Ubuntu, Fedora and relatives,
Alpine, WSL2), and wincan.exe, the candle window, which runs on the Windows desktop.  They talk over TLS; each
client proves itself with a key of its own, which the server's administrator authorizes (candled(8), SECURITY).
There is no plain-HTTP fallback in either program: plain HTTP is for this computer only.

The server, from the release tarball
------------------------------------
Needs a C compiler, make and the development files of sqlite3, libcurl and OpenSSL:

    Debian, Ubuntu:   sudo apt install build-essential libsqlite3-dev libcurl4-openssl-dev libssl-dev
    Fedora, RHEL:     sudo dnf install gcc make sqlite-devel libcurl-devel openssl-devel
    Alpine:           sudo apk add build-base sqlite-dev curl-dev openssl-dev

Then the usual:

    ./configure && make && make check && sudo make install

The installer script does all of that and the rest (the service user, the server's key, the service):

    sudo sh linux/install-candled.sh            # system-wide: /usr/local, a candled user, a service
    sh linux/install-candled.sh --user          # for you only, under ~/.local, no root
    sudo sh linux/install-candled.sh --uninstall [--purge]

It starts the service with systemd, or OpenRC on Alpine; on WSL2 without systemd it says how to start candled
(turn systemd on in /etc/wsl.conf, or start it by hand or from the [boot] section).  See
linux/install-candled.sh --help.

On Debian and Ubuntu there is a package:  dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc -b  makes wincan_*.deb: the server, and the
Windows files with their installer (for WSL2).  contrib/wincan.spec (RPM) and contrib/APKBUILD (Alpine) are
written but not tested.

configure options of note:  --prefix, --sysconfdir (default /usr/local/etc; the packages use /etc), --localstatedir,
--with-systemdsystemunitdir=DIR (install the unit there), --disable-windows (do not build wincan.exe), WINCC=...
(the MinGW-w64 compiler).  make install installs candled.conf in sysconfdir if there is none; make uninstall removes
it only if it is still the sample.  The usual targets: all check install install-strip uninstall installcheck dist
distcheck clean distclean.  ./configure --help lists the rest (the standard GNU options: --bindir, --mandir, DESTDIR
for a staged install, VPATH builds, and so on).

From a checkout of the repository: ./bootstrap first (needs autoconf and automake; HACKING).

The window, on Windows
----------------------
Unpack the Windows zip of the release (wincan.exe, install.ps1, uninstall.ps1, install.cmd).  You do not need the
installer: run wincan.exe.  The first time it asks where the candles come from, offers to make this computer's key
(one Yes or No) and puts   candled --authorize SHA256:...   on the clipboard for the server's administrator.

The installer adds the Start Menu entry and Win+R, and can do the same things up front.  In PowerShell:

    powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File install.ps1 -Server https://HOST:8766 -ServerPin SHA256:... -Launch

-Server and -ServerPin come from the server's administrator (the installer of candled prints them).  The installer
makes this computer's key and prints its fingerprint; the administrator runs   candled --authorize SHA256:...   and
wincan connects.  No administrator rights are needed on Windows.  Start wincan from the Start Menu or with Win+R
"wincan".  -DryRun says what the installer would do.  Remove with uninstall.ps1.

wincan.exe is also built by make on a system with MinGW-w64 and installed in $(datadir)/wincan/windows beside the
installer, so that on WSL2 the installer can be run from Linux:

    powershell.exe -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File "$(wslpath -w /usr/local/share/wincan/windows/install.ps1)" -Server https://localhost:8766

WSL2: a candled in WSL2 that listens on 127.0.0.1 is reached from Windows as localhost (WSL2's default).
